## Step 1: Cast the Bronze Bearings (The Friction Solution)
A machine cannot function if moving parts grind against static parts. You need low-friction pivot points.

   1. The Mold: Carve two matching pairs of hollow, semi-cylindrical troughs into a soft block of sandstone.
   2. The Pour: Smelt your copper and tin to create bronze [0.2]. Pour the molten liquid into the sandstone molds.
   3. The Assembly: Once cooled, you will have four smooth bronze brackets. When greased with animal fat (tallow), these brackets act as heavy-duty journals where your main wooden drive shaft can spin with minimal friction.

------------------------------
## Step 2: Construct the Flywheel (Storing Kinetic Energy)
Your leg can only push down; it cannot pull up. To keep the machine spinning on the upward return stroke, you need a flywheel.

   1. Shape the Disk: Construct a large, perfectly round wheel out of wet clay, roughly 50 cm (20 inches) in diameter and 10 cm thick. Pack it dense and heavy.
   2. Fire It: Fire the wheel in your Crossdraft Kiln past 1000°C to vitrify the clay into heavy, solid ceramic.
   3. Mount the Shaft: Insert a straight, sturdy hardwood axle perfectly through the center hole of the wheel. Secure it tightly using wooden wedges and tree resin glue.

------------------------------
## Step 3: Frame and Mechanical Linkage
Now, you assemble the frame to harness the power of your legs.

* 
* The Frame: Anchor two heavy, vertical wooden posts deeply into the dirt floor of your shelter. Mount your bronze bearings to the tops of these posts. Lay your drive shaft (with the flywheel attached) horizontally across the bearings.
* The Treadle: Anchor a long, thick wooden board to the ground using a leather hinge or flexible vine so it acts as a foot pedal.
* The Drive Rope: Tie your high-tensile flax or bark rope [0.2] to the tip of the foot pedal. Wrap the rope tightly around the drive shaft three times, then tie the upper end of the rope to a flexible, springy tree branch mounted overhead.
* 

------------------------------
## How the Machine Translates Force
When you stomp down on the foot pedal, the rope pulls downward, aggressively spinning the main shaft and the heavy flywheel. When your foot reaches the bottom, the flexible overhead branch snaps back up, pulling the rope upward and resetting the pedal.
Normally, this would reverse the rotation, but the heavy flywheel stores the rotational momentum, carrying the machine through the dead zones of your pedaling rhythm.
------------------------------
## What This Machine Can Now Build for You
With this lathe operational, you have moved from a simple tool-maker to a mechanical engineer. You can hold a sharp piece of flint or bronze against a spinning piece of wood to instantly manufacture:

* 
* Pulley Blocks: To lift multi-ton stones or logs effortlessly.
* Precision Gears: Interlocking wooden teeth to build waterwheels and windmills.
* Pistons & Valves: To build air bellows that pump massive amounts of oxygen into your smelting furnaces, allowing you to move from copper to melting Iron.
